a panel discussion, and then we'll open up for Q&A. All right. So, today we are focused

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:33.119
on diving into human-in-the-loop analytics, or HITL. It's a concept that's redefining

00:02:33.119 --> 00:02:39.199
how we approach automation, analytics, and AI readiness. So, despite all the progress

00:02:39.199 --> 00:02:45.279
in tools and models, the truth is that data prep still consumes about 60 to 80 percent

00:02:45.279 --> 00:02:50.960
of analytics project time, and that's where human-in-the-loop assistants really create

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:56.759
measurable value. They shorten our time to insight. They help us keep domain experts

00:02:56.759 --> 00:03:03.320
firmly in control. We're also seeing tremendous energy around AI adoption, but readiness and

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:10.960
trust remain kind of uneven. HITL doesn't replace human expertise, as most people think.

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:17.000
It really operationalizes it. It helps us strike a balance between automation, accountability,

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:28.360
and create a path to scale analytics responsibly. So, let's take a look at where organizations

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:35.080
stand today. A lot of this data comes from TDWI research, and our latest data shows that

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:42.000
only 30 percent of organizations have leadership fully aligned on AI strategy and implementation.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:47.440
Another 25 percent of organizations understand that AI's impact is there, but they don't

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:54.279
really understand how to operationalize it. This is where guided HITL approaches actually

00:03:54.279 --> 00:04:00.839
come into place. They help us deliver pragmatic progress. Only 16 percent of organizations

00:04:00.839 --> 00:04:06.279
are actively executing AI strategies at scale, and the majority are still in planning or

00:04:06.279 --> 00:04:13.399
doing some kind of proof of concept. Perhaps the most telling is the culture of AI trust.

00:04:13.399 --> 00:04:18.920
It's still neutral across most companies, and there's a recognition of potential, but

00:04:18.920 --> 00:04:23.600
there's hesitation around accountability. And human-in-the-loop methods, they help us

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:30.720
strengthen things like transparency and governance during this first phase of maturity. They

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:35.920
make it possible for leaders to move forward confidently while maintaining oversight and

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:44.880
ethical boundaries. So, if we look past the strategy layer, we'll find some persistent

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:52.799
gaps in foundational data practices, especially around quality definitions and accountability.

00:04:52.799 --> 00:04:58.600
In our research, we found that 38 percent of organizations have no defined data quality

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:05.799
strategy, while another 33 percent rely on IT-led approaches without getting wider stakeholder

00:05:05.799 --> 00:05:12.119
buy-in. Even more concerning, 41 percent of organizations report no company-wide data

00:05:12.119 --> 00:05:18.480
quality training and half have no shared data definitions across business domains. So, these

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:23.959
are areas where human-in-the-loop oversight can make a tangible difference. These types

00:05:23.959 --> 00:05:30.959
of checkpoints allow experts to review outputs, enforce our shared standards, and then validate

00:05:30.959 --> 00:05:39.079
that automated processes align with the organizational expectations. In short, HITL becomes the quality

00:05:39.079 --> 00:05:45.000
control layer that many organizations lack formally. It helps enforce governance in practice

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:56.839
before policy catches up. So, now let's look at the technology landscape. While AI platforms

00:05:56.839 --> 00:06:03.959
are proliferating, the ecosystem is still forming, and it's far from finished. Only

00:06:03.959 --> 00:06:10.760
33 percent of organizations have a set and communicated ecosystem strategy, and a mere

00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:17.239
12 percent are measuring outcomes against it. But on the upside, we have 54 percent

00:06:17.239 --> 00:06:23.399
of their organizations report active machine learning or AI support, and 51 percent of

00:06:23.399 --> 00:06:30.040
organizations are piloting gen AI initiatives. Integration is actually improving as well.

00:06:30.040 --> 00:06:36.440
52 percent of organizations have APIs and connectors in place, but only 21 percent report

00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:44.359
really having a unified data view. We still see silos as a major obstacle. Here again,

00:06:44.359 --> 00:06:51.000
human-in-the-loop validation adds stability during this transition. It also requires human

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:58.200
review of certain things like data joins, transformation logic, AI outputs, and organizations

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:03.640
can prevent misinterpretation across data sources while integration and virtualization

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:12.119
technologies continue to mature. So, let's talk about where organizations are actually

00:07:12.119 --> 00:07:20.200
investing in 2025 and how those priorities actually intersect with HITL. The top analytics

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:27.320
and AI priorities include focusing on self-service analytics. 30 percent, 37 percent of organizations

00:07:28.359 --> 00:07:33.000
basically reported they're focusing on self-service analytics, and they want to

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:39.079
support empowering business users with guided, explainable assistance. Also, no surprise,

00:07:39.079 --> 00:07:46.119
gen AI is also up there. We're looking for human oversight, ensuring responsible implementation.

00:07:46.119 --> 00:07:52.760
There's also reporting of around 32 percent of focus on data availability and literacy,

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:58.679
emphasizing both access and understanding. But then on the data management side, we have

00:07:58.679 --> 00:08:04.760
priorities where organizations are emphasizing automating routine tasks. About 34 percent of

00:08:04.760 --> 00:08:10.600
organizations reported this, but they want to do so under human supervision. We also had

00:08:10.600 --> 00:08:16.760
organizations, around 31 percent of them, focus on catalog and metadata management where they're

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:22.440
wanting to improve discovery and lineage tracking. Governance frameworks also ranked up there where

00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:27.959
they're wanting to look at embedding human checkpoints. And then lastly, advanced analytics

00:08:27.959 --> 00:08:34.919
support. About 42 percent of organizations looked at pairing AI acceleration with human validation.

00:08:35.719 --> 00:08:39.239
In essence, HITL analytics is the connective tissue

00:08:39.239 --> 00:08:44.599
among these priorities, and it helps make automation safe, interpretable, and operationable.

00:08:48.520 --> 00:08:53.640
So what does human in the loop analytics really look like in the real world? We've seen a couple

00:08:53.640 --> 00:09:00.599
of examples through the prior slides, but AI-driven assistance will take on repetitive

00:09:00.599 --> 00:09:07.320
mechanical tasks such as schema inference, key detection, anomaly flagging, and also code

00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:14.039
scaffolding. Meanwhile, users and humans have to stay responsible for the context-rich steps such

00:09:14.039 --> 00:09:20.280
as defining your data transformations, the sampling strategies, handling outliers, and setting what

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:27.479
those acceptance criteria are going to be. Every action is captured for traceability from prompts

00:09:27.479 --> 00:09:35.719
and generated code to decisions and approval. In an HITL ecosystem, for example, each interaction

00:09:35.719 --> 00:09:42.599
can create a reproducible artifact with full logging and auditability. This ensures transparency

00:09:42.599 --> 00:09:48.440
not only for analysts but also for governance and compliance teams. It's the best of both worlds

00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:55.799
where automation is focused on where speed matters and human oversight is placed where judgment counts.

00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:04.359
So one of the most critical strategic discussions right now is determining

00:10:04.359 --> 00:10:11.400
what to automate and what to keep under human control. Tasks that are consistent, rule-based,

00:10:11.479 --> 00:10:18.119
and low-risk are ideal for automation. This includes some typical things like data type

00:10:18.119 --> 00:10:25.080
detection and classification, missing value handling, understanding joint paths, maybe even

00:10:25.080 --> 00:10:32.119
a recommendation for a specific chart or visualization, creating summary statistics

00:10:32.119 --> 00:10:37.400
as part of exploratory data analysis, or even maybe the first draft of code generation.

00:10:37.880 --> 00:10:42.919
These are some building blocks where speed brings efficiency without really risking

00:10:42.919 --> 00:10:48.679
some kind of interpretive error. But other tasks, those things that involve judgment,

00:10:48.679 --> 00:10:53.799
understanding ethics, maybe even business meaning, these have to stay human-driven.

00:10:53.799 --> 00:11:01.159
This means that defining metrics, dealing with bias and ethics assessments, interpretation of

00:11:01.159 --> 00:11:07.000
compliance, and those acceptance thresholds, these really need to be part of what a human does.

00:11:07.640 --> 00:11:13.640
Until we have things like data quality maturity and training and we are able to catch up, human

00:11:13.640 --> 00:11:19.960
involvement remains indispensable in these areas. Remember, the goal isn't to automate everything,

00:11:19.960 --> 00:11:27.320
it's to automate well and to do so responsibly. So if we bring the big picture together, we're

00:11:27.320 --> 00:11:33.880
at a moment where we're excited for new things, but we also have to be cautious. Automation is

00:11:33.880 --> 00:11:40.039
obviously advancing very quickly, but maturity, governance, and trust are evolving more slowly.

00:11:40.679 --> 00:11:47.239
HITL analytics helps bridge that divide. It allows us to move faster and safer,

00:11:47.239 --> 00:11:53.159
and we can automate what we can, but we have to anchor all of our decisions in human oversight.

