And while there is certainly speed increases that happen with these tools, I think the

00:39:58.216 --> 00:40:01.175
whole point of this discussion is it doesn't remove our need to be involved.

00:40:01.455 --> 00:40:05.175
And so I think sometimes it is pitfall saying, OK, like I can just like offload this

00:40:05.175 --> 00:40:07.735
entire thing to this machine to go do.

00:40:08.295 --> 00:40:12.816
But really, it's like let's work together with the machine, and that is kind of the best

00:40:12.816 --> 00:40:16.256
of both worlds. And so when we talk about the practices to help avoid them, I think

00:40:16.256 --> 00:40:20.536
part of it is kind of what we showed in the demo where it's, hey, instead of instead of

00:40:20.776 --> 00:40:24.336
here's a prompt, go work on this for 30 minutes and come back to me, it's here's a

00:40:24.336 --> 00:40:28.936
prompt. You're going to come back to me in a minute with some new suggestions and things

00:40:28.936 --> 00:40:33.135
we could look at, and I'm going to stay here lockstep the entire time so that it's so

00:40:33.135 --> 00:40:36.616
that I'm an active participant instead of like a distant observer of what's happening.

00:40:36.616 --> 00:40:40.855
And I think that that practice of being an active participant in these workflows is the

00:40:40.855 --> 00:40:41.776
thing that makes the difference.

00:40:44.376 --> 00:40:45.455
Excellent, thank you.

00:40:45.975 --> 00:40:48.616
All right, let's see about our next question here.

00:40:51.855 --> 00:40:58.056
All right, so based on your experience, which EDA and data prep tasks are the priority to

00:40:58.056 --> 00:41:04.256
automate? I can I can take this first one, I think there's there's the common.

00:41:05.215 --> 00:41:10.376
Kind of adage that 80 percent of data work or whatever, whatever high percentage you want to

00:41:10.376 --> 00:41:17.576
sign is spent in this sort of like EDA data prep stage, and I think a lot of that is just we

00:41:17.576 --> 00:41:19.576
need to familiarize ourself with what's there.

00:41:19.576 --> 00:41:25.376
And as much as we all love to to think about and dream about clean data, in a lot of

00:41:25.376 --> 00:41:27.775
cases, what we're working with is not clean.

00:41:27.775 --> 00:41:31.376
There's errors, there's anomalies, there's things that need to be considered, there's

00:41:31.735 --> 00:41:36.415
caveats. And so I think that process and I found that tools like data bot and other kind of

00:41:36.415 --> 00:41:41.496
similar things can be really helpful at just kind of understanding the big picture really

00:41:41.496 --> 00:41:44.256
quickly and understanding where the rough edges are.

00:41:44.256 --> 00:41:48.576
Right. Like, oh, in the in the in the weather data that we're looking at.

00:41:48.576 --> 00:41:53.535
Right. Like I was looking at this the other day and data bot was was like, look, there's

00:41:53.535 --> 00:41:57.616
there's a lot of entry points for temperature that are negative forty nine point nine.

00:41:57.616 --> 00:42:01.016
And I don't know if that's because that's just a really common temperature or maybe that's

00:42:01.016 --> 00:42:03.616
like a missing value code that we need to investigate.

00:42:03.616 --> 00:42:08.456
Just things that like I might not have necessarily noticed right away, but within five

00:42:08.456 --> 00:42:12.175
minutes of working with data bot, we had come up with, OK, here's a couple of things that

00:42:12.175 --> 00:42:13.376
just don't seem quite right.

00:42:13.376 --> 00:42:17.535
We saw this really low temperature spike in January or this really high temperature spike

00:42:17.535 --> 00:42:20.016
in January that doesn't seem typical.

00:42:20.016 --> 00:42:22.056
So this could represent noise in the data.

00:42:22.056 --> 00:42:26.215
We also so I think getting that kind of initial high level overview of the data and then

00:42:26.215 --> 00:42:30.775
also just becoming familiar with every every piece of data has its rough edges that, you

00:42:30.775 --> 00:42:32.696
know, it's data quality issues.

00:42:32.696 --> 00:42:36.936
These kinds of tools, I think, can be really helpful at just getting through that quickly

00:42:36.936 --> 00:42:41.335
so that then we have the right mental model to start asking the right questions.

00:42:41.335 --> 00:42:46.095
I think sometimes I've been stuck in the past where I spend hours and hours just trying

00:42:46.095 --> 00:42:51.295
to figure out, OK, what can I and can't I ask this data based on what it does and does

00:42:51.295 --> 00:42:54.056
not provide? And now we can get to that point.

00:42:54.056 --> 00:42:58.016
We can get through that part much, much more quickly and get to the point where we're actually

00:42:58.016 --> 00:43:01.136
asking the meaningful questions because we have a correct understanding of what we're

00:43:01.136 --> 00:43:02.136
working with.

00:43:04.136 --> 00:43:10.376
Yeah, just one comment, yeah, so I think for me also the visualization also is a great

00:43:10.376 --> 00:43:14.855
example, we can probably do more automation because, you know, those things are a little

00:43:14.855 --> 00:43:19.496
bit low risk because it's more for us humans to draw the insights.

00:43:19.496 --> 00:43:24.016
And sometimes, you know, I have an experience before when I was a data scientist.

00:43:24.016 --> 00:43:28.775
So it's pretty much a similar piece of code that I always borrow from the previous

00:43:28.775 --> 00:43:31.855
notebook. But those searching really takes time.

00:43:31.855 --> 00:43:35.775
In the end, you generate pretty much similar style of visualization, right?

00:43:35.775 --> 00:43:38.215
And it gives you the nice looking stuff.

00:43:38.215 --> 00:43:42.855
But it takes a lot of time to really have a lot of this decorations on this

00:43:42.855 --> 00:43:47.576
visuals, right? You have put the labels where you have put this legends and all the

00:43:47.576 --> 00:43:48.775
color and all that stuff.

00:43:49.735 --> 00:43:52.496
I think those detailed work doesn't really add value.

00:43:53.095 --> 00:43:58.896
The more value comes from the visualization itself, the insight that these visualizations

00:43:58.896 --> 00:44:03.855
show. So I think we can automate those type of work that will save us a lot of time,

00:44:03.855 --> 00:44:08.295
right? Because in the end, you just want to look at the insights out of those visuals,

00:44:08.295 --> 00:44:09.175
right?

00:44:09.175 --> 00:44:10.376
Yeah, I think that one's huge.

00:44:10.376 --> 00:44:14.816
And I'm glad you brought that up because that's been one of my favorite use cases of

00:44:14.816 --> 00:44:16.456
just exactly that.

00:44:16.456 --> 00:44:21.415
Like I know what visualization I want to make, but I always have to go back and like

00:44:21.415 --> 00:44:26.496
re-Google the right syntax to get it to look the way that I want so that I can make the

00:44:26.496 --> 00:44:27.735
decision I'm making and move on.

00:44:28.095 --> 00:44:32.256
And I found that AI is a tremendous help there where instead of needing to go and like

00:44:32.256 --> 00:44:36.456
Google and then figure out how to work through that back into my code, I can just say,

00:44:36.456 --> 00:44:40.376
wait a minute, actually, let's add this is the y-axis and this is the x-axis or whatever,

00:44:40.376 --> 00:44:44.335
right? Whatever request, it's a very natural, like help me build a visualization the way

00:44:44.335 --> 00:44:45.936
that I see it in my mind process.

00:44:46.775 --> 00:44:48.775
And it's, yeah, I completely agree.

00:44:48.775 --> 00:44:52.616
I think visualization is a huge area where we see this have a high impact.

00:44:54.816 --> 00:45:00.775
Excellent. So a lot of the repeatable, more complex EDA tasks can really benefit from

00:45:01.215 --> 00:45:02.456
this type of approach.

00:45:03.696 --> 00:45:05.775
All right, let's go to our next question.

00:45:06.775 --> 00:45:13.456
OK, what human-in-the-loop controls exist to approve or roll back AI-generated steps?

00:45:19.215 --> 00:45:22.896
Yeah, I can I can take this one, I thought it's sorry, I'll be brief.

00:45:22.896 --> 00:45:27.696
I think we kind of saw it in the demo a little bit where it's always asked for permission

00:45:27.696 --> 00:45:29.215
before code is executed.

00:45:29.535 --> 00:45:34.535
And then Shun brought up earlier, and I completely agree with this, that we have a lot of

00:45:34.816 --> 00:45:38.415
the existing sort of scaffolding in place for some of these things, version control and

00:45:38.415 --> 00:45:43.855
other tools that are actually complement and work very well with some of these AI tools

00:45:43.855 --> 00:45:50.335
where, OK, I'm going to build out this process, but everything's going to be tracked in

00:45:50.335 --> 00:45:54.175
version control. There's logs that I can put in place at the bedrock level.

00:45:54.175 --> 00:45:57.576
So there's kind of this wide range of ways that I'm going to keep track of what's

00:45:57.576 --> 00:46:01.016
happening, roll back or revert to things as needed.

00:46:01.535 --> 00:46:07.735
I think one of the key advantages that you have with data analysis and data science type

00:46:07.735 --> 00:46:13.696
work is that it's typically very different in some fundamental ways from traditional

00:46:13.696 --> 00:46:18.175
software engineering, where I'm not like making this huge, I'm not typically in data

00:46:18.175 --> 00:46:22.655
science work, making this huge refactor of a massive code base and then recompiling that

00:46:22.655 --> 00:46:25.056
into a program and then checking to see if there's bugs.

00:46:26.095 --> 00:46:28.415
Instead, I'm like interacting in real time.

00:46:28.775 --> 00:46:32.136
And so the way that the way that DataBot does this, I didn't have a chance to kind of

00:46:32.136 --> 00:46:38.975
show this, but the way that we do it there is I might go down some specific branch of

00:46:38.975 --> 00:46:40.175
exploration of my data.

00:46:40.175 --> 00:46:44.376
You know, I'm looking at the weather data and now all of a sudden I'm way in the weeds

00:46:44.376 --> 00:46:46.655
looking at temperature of a specific region.

00:46:46.975 --> 00:46:49.775
And I just realized, like, this actually wasn't that valuable.

00:46:49.775 --> 00:46:52.256
I mean, it was interesting, but there's nothing there's no signal here.

00:46:52.576 --> 00:46:54.335
Whatever the case is, it's really easy.

00:46:54.335 --> 00:46:57.456
DataBot gives you this view where I can take a step back and I can look at the total

00:46:57.496 --> 00:47:01.376
conversation history and go back to any sort of previous point in that conversation and

00:47:01.376 --> 00:47:04.696
say, actually, like, let's branch, let's let's take a different direction from here.

00:47:04.696 --> 00:47:07.696
And I can go back to that point and then like rebranch the conversation into a

00:47:07.696 --> 00:47:11.696
different direction. So you can end up with this like fairly complex tree of things that

00:47:11.696 --> 00:47:16.016
I've explored, but I can always kind of navigate back through it if I need to unwind

00:47:16.016 --> 00:47:17.215
or go explore something else.

00:47:19.775 --> 00:47:26.136
Yeah, right. I think on that, I see a lot of enterprises already actually implemented

00:47:26.136 --> 00:47:31.735
their approval workflow, so they have probably different steps to approve for code or for

00:47:31.735 --> 00:47:35.095
some kind of decisions for the deployment and all that.

00:47:35.095 --> 00:47:36.936
So everything still there.

00:47:36.936 --> 00:47:44.056
You just need to add maybe the AI generated code or data transformations or the

00:47:44.056 --> 00:47:49.496
processings into that workflow, maybe tagging those AI generated assets differently than

00:47:49.496 --> 00:47:51.335
normally the human generated assets.

00:47:51.335 --> 00:47:55.576
And also, plus that, I would say there are also some other controls you can do to roll

00:47:55.576 --> 00:48:00.136
back whatever AI generated steps, the first one being the data versioning.

00:48:00.136 --> 00:48:04.215
So, for example, Amazon F3, we have the data versioning capability.

00:48:04.215 --> 00:48:08.775
So even you've done something, you've made some mistakes, you generate a new version of

00:48:08.775 --> 00:48:11.975
data, you still have this older version to back you up.

00:48:11.975 --> 00:48:18.295
You can go back to your older data and redo the work that you've done previously.

00:48:18.376 --> 00:48:22.936
And notebook versioning also, it's pretty standard.

00:48:22.936 --> 00:48:27.576
So you generate a new notebook, but you find that maybe something is not quite right, you

00:48:27.576 --> 00:48:30.616
can go back to your old notebook and still do the work.

00:48:31.415 --> 00:48:36.936
And same thing with the code itself, the Git control, you have all this full Git history,

00:48:36.936 --> 00:48:38.936
so you can take a look at what has been changed.

00:48:39.816 --> 00:48:46.136
Yeah, so I think there are a lot of ways you can implement this rollback and human control.

00:48:46.136 --> 00:48:46.636
Yeah.

00:48:48.535 --> 00:48:48.855
All right.

00:48:48.855 --> 00:48:51.256
Sounds like we have traceability steps.

00:48:51.256 --> 00:48:55.576
We have the ability to use data control, code control.

00:48:55.576 --> 00:48:58.696
So all of these can help us roll back AI generated steps.

00:49:00.136 --> 00:49:01.735
Let's look at the next question.

00:49:05.016 --> 00:49:11.016
And I think this is kind of similar, but I think there's one part of it that we may not

00:49:11.016 --> 00:49:11.655
have addressed.

00:49:12.056 --> 00:49:16.936
How do you version and track prompt code changes across projects for governance?

00:49:19.335 --> 00:49:21.496
I can start a little bit on this.

00:49:21.496 --> 00:49:25.896
I think for me, I want to relate to the Amazon Bedrock again here.

00:49:26.935 --> 00:49:32.536
So Amazon Bedrock, for example, it has a unique feature that supports the prompt management

00:49:32.536 --> 00:49:36.376
with versioning, aliases, and a flow orchestration.

00:49:36.376 --> 00:49:42.455
So I think in the AI world, prompt is really the key.

00:49:42.455 --> 00:49:45.256
Prompt means the ability to ask the correct question.

00:49:45.256 --> 00:49:50.215
So I think the assets you have in the very beginning are those prompts.

00:49:50.215 --> 00:49:52.455
For different projects, you may have different prompts.

00:49:52.455 --> 00:49:58.855
So Amazon Bedrock actually allows you to manage all this prompt you have for different projects.

00:49:58.855 --> 00:50:06.055
It has proper tagging, has proper logging tools behind the scenes, and it can also have

00:50:06.215 --> 00:50:09.496
guardrails implemented to make sure you have the correct prompt.

00:50:09.496 --> 00:50:14.376
You have a legal prompt to fit into your business purposes.

00:50:15.896 --> 00:50:21.176
So I think this prompt management, to me, is actually a key in this now large-length

00:50:21.176 --> 00:50:22.536
model world.

00:50:27.896 --> 00:50:28.376
I agree.

00:50:28.376 --> 00:50:32.855
I don't have anything further to add on Shun's comments, but I think that all is what we

00:50:32.855 --> 00:50:33.815
see on our end as well.

00:50:36.616 --> 00:50:37.176
All right.

00:50:37.176 --> 00:50:39.975
So I think let's see what other questions we have here.

00:50:39.975 --> 00:50:40.296
OK.

00:50:40.296 --> 00:50:43.496
I think that takes us to the end of our panel discussion.

00:50:43.496 --> 00:50:45.016
So we'll go back to Andrew.

00:50:46.776 --> 00:50:47.256
Terrific.

00:50:47.256 --> 00:50:49.416
Thank you all for that great conversation there.

00:50:49.416 --> 00:50:51.975
It's time for audience questions here.

00:50:51.975 --> 00:50:54.695
And we have some great questions from the audience.

00:50:54.695 --> 00:50:56.455
So we'll start with this one for James.

00:50:57.656 --> 00:51:01.095
And if anyone else has something to add afterwards, please go ahead and do so.

00:51:01.656 --> 00:51:09.416
So James, this person asks, how does AI assistance for EDA-type tasks differ from traditional

00:51:09.416 --> 00:51:11.416
software engineering AI assistance?

00:51:13.416 --> 00:51:18.776
Yeah, this has been an interesting question to think about.

00:51:21.176 --> 00:51:25.735
On the posit side, our background with the RStudio IDE and developing that for a number

00:51:25.735 --> 00:51:31.016
of years and really working very closely with this community of data scientists or

00:51:31.016 --> 00:51:35.416
statisticians or, again, whatever kind of title you want to assign them has, I think,

00:51:35.416 --> 00:51:40.376
informed us very, very well of what their workflows and what those workflows look like.

00:51:40.376 --> 00:51:44.935
Many of us at the company worked in a former life as data science practitioners.

00:51:45.735 --> 00:51:50.935
And there's these subtle differences where, and I kind of alluded to it in a previous

00:51:50.935 --> 00:51:55.176
comment, but you have, in traditional software engineering, you're often working with static

00:51:55.176 --> 00:51:59.095
code, large code bases, lots of interdependencies.

00:51:59.095 --> 00:52:04.616
And so you look at a lot of these, kind of the discourse that exists with generative

00:52:04.616 --> 00:52:09.815
AI assistance in software engineering, and you see things like challenges with context

00:52:09.815 --> 00:52:15.735
windows because of large code bases and how do we keep AI on task for large tasks that

00:52:15.735 --> 00:52:19.176
span across multiple function definitions and calls across different things.

00:52:19.176 --> 00:52:24.455
And then at the end of the day, you're creating the code kind of based off of these static

00:52:24.455 --> 00:52:29.496
files and then compiling or building that code into some sort of a final solution.

00:52:30.215 --> 00:52:36.136
Whereas data science is far more interactive in a way, like we walked through in the demo

00:52:36.136 --> 00:52:40.136
where I have a real-time interpreter, I'm writing some R code, I'm writing some Python

00:52:40.136 --> 00:52:44.536
code or whatever framework I want to use, and then I'm getting immediate feedback that

00:52:44.536 --> 00:52:46.136
code's executing right away.

00:52:46.136 --> 00:52:49.896
And then I'm responding to that with some new code that's written or I'm editing the

00:52:49.896 --> 00:52:51.335
previous code and rerunning it again.

00:52:51.335 --> 00:52:53.735
So it's this very tight loop that's happening.

00:52:54.536 --> 00:52:58.616
And I think when you look at tools like Data Bot and some of these other things, they're

00:52:58.616 --> 00:53:01.095
built around this idea of immediate execution.

00:53:01.095 --> 00:53:05.735
I write code, I execute it right away, I look at the results, and then I make a decision.

00:53:05.735 --> 00:53:09.176
Maybe that decision is to write more code or generate a visualization or ask a different

00:53:09.176 --> 00:53:12.055
question, but then that loop just keeps repeating itself.

00:53:12.055 --> 00:53:18.296
And so we recognize that that's the way that most of these data practitioners work, and

00:53:18.296 --> 00:53:21.815
that's where they find their most value and their highest level of productivity is in

00:53:21.815 --> 00:53:27.176
this very rapid real-time feedback loop with some sort of active interpreter.

00:53:27.176 --> 00:53:31.095
And so the tools that we've built and the tools that we see emerging follow that same

00:53:31.656 --> 00:53:32.855
design philosophy.

00:53:32.855 --> 00:53:38.136
We write code, execute it immediately, look at results, and then make a decision based

00:53:38.136 --> 00:53:38.376
on that.

00:53:39.335 --> 00:53:43.016
Maybe it's a subtle difference, but I think it's a fairly fundamental difference in the

00:53:43.016 --> 00:53:48.616
way that these tools are designed to work versus a more, quote-unquote, kind of traditional

00:53:48.616 --> 00:53:50.136
software engineering style agent.

00:53:54.376 --> 00:53:54.935
All right, terrific.

00:53:54.935 --> 00:53:56.616
We'll move on to the next question here.

00:53:56.616 --> 00:54:01.735
And Sean, this one's, I think, directed towards you, so if you don't mind stepping up here.

00:54:01.735 --> 00:54:08.136
This person asks, how do organizations choose LLMs in such a fast-developing space, and

00:54:08.136 --> 00:54:10.136
what does Amazon offer for this?

00:54:11.016 --> 00:54:11.575
Sure, Andrew.

00:54:11.575 --> 00:54:13.575
Yeah, I think that's a great question.

00:54:13.575 --> 00:54:19.735
I think a lot of, especially now, a lot of customers are facing this kind of issue, is

00:54:19.735 --> 00:54:26.136
that with so many model providers on the market, which one actually we should choose?

00:54:26.136 --> 00:54:32.536
I think the short answer from AWS is that our philosophy is the choice plus flexibility.

00:54:33.335 --> 00:54:38.136
So we believe that there is no single model that will rule them all.

00:54:39.016 --> 00:54:44.776
So maybe today, there may be one or two or three models that are better than the others,

00:54:44.776 --> 00:54:45.496
but who knows?

00:54:45.496 --> 00:54:50.855
Maybe tomorrow or next month, there are going to be another model providers who have a big

00:54:50.855 --> 00:54:54.215
breakthrough and break this kind of balance again.

00:54:54.215 --> 00:54:59.975
So we don't know, actually, which model really is the best over the long run.

00:55:00.695 --> 00:55:08.055
So that's why we actually have this layered offering from AWS, the first one being the

00:55:08.055 --> 00:55:13.896
Bedrock, which is a single API access to multiple different model, foundation models.

00:55:14.455 --> 00:55:17.656
So right now, for example, we have Anthropic Cloud models.

00:55:17.656 --> 00:55:19.575
We have Lama models.

00:55:19.575 --> 00:55:24.536
We have AWS-owned Nova models, Mistro, Cohere, AI21.

00:55:24.536 --> 00:55:26.776
Even open AI models now we support.

00:55:27.735 --> 00:55:34.695
Also, we provide customers with different modalities of models, such as text-based models,

00:55:34.695 --> 00:55:38.776
image model, audio model, even video models as well.

00:55:39.416 --> 00:55:46.455
And plus that, we know that some organizations, they may not use those models directly because

00:55:46.455 --> 00:55:49.815
they have their own business logics, business backgrounds.

00:55:49.815 --> 00:55:53.496
They want to tune the model by themselves with their own enterprise data.

00:55:53.496 --> 00:55:59.335
So we allow this enterprise to have this capability by providing the customization

00:55:59.335 --> 00:56:04.215
service for them, meaning that they can choose a base model, but feed the model with their

00:56:04.215 --> 00:56:09.416
own data and fine-tune it towards their own business purposes.

00:56:10.376 --> 00:56:18.776
So also, if you're an organization or startup who have really strong developers or machine

00:56:18.776 --> 00:56:25.095
learning engineers, you can choose Amazon SageMaker, which provides even more knobs

00:56:25.095 --> 00:56:26.776
to tune and customize.

00:56:26.776 --> 00:56:31.496
You can even build the model from the ground up, from the pre-training stage, or you can

00:56:31.496 --> 00:56:32.376
do the fine-tuning.

00:56:32.376 --> 00:56:37.016
You can do model distillation, model hosting, or distributor training.

00:56:37.016 --> 00:56:40.455
So we allow you to have all these different kinds of choices.

00:56:41.016 --> 00:56:47.176
So yeah, I mean, the answer is that we provide the customers choice they want to have.

00:56:47.176 --> 00:56:49.176
We don't provide any single model.

00:56:49.176 --> 00:56:51.496
We just give them all the choices.

00:56:51.496 --> 00:56:52.055
Yeah.

00:56:55.735 --> 00:56:56.536
All right, fantastic.

00:56:56.536 --> 00:57:01.256
I think we have time for one final question here, and James, we'll go back to you for

00:57:01.256 --> 00:57:01.896
this one.

00:57:01.896 --> 00:57:06.695
This came in during the discussion, so maybe you can expand on some things that you've

00:57:06.695 --> 00:57:07.975
already mentioned.

00:57:07.975 --> 00:57:12.855
This person asked, what are the inherently human attributes that provide the most value

00:57:12.855 --> 00:57:14.215
to these AI workflows?

00:57:14.776 --> 00:57:21.095
Yeah, again, kind of going back to some of the comments I made earlier, I think this

00:57:21.095 --> 00:57:27.656
is a really great opportunity, as we consider the ways in which AI are enhancing our efficiency

00:57:27.656 --> 00:57:34.055
in certain areas, to just reinvestigate, like, what do we bring to the table that AI isn't

00:57:34.055 --> 00:57:34.695
bringing to the table?

00:57:34.695 --> 00:57:39.656
And how do we bring those two things together in a way that moves work forward in new and

00:57:39.656 --> 00:57:40.455
exciting ways?

00:57:40.455 --> 00:57:44.376
And like I mentioned a little bit earlier, I think in my mind, a lot of this comes down

00:57:44.376 --> 00:57:50.296
to our own just sense of curiosity and wonder and our ability to think creatively.

00:57:50.296 --> 00:57:59.256
As fantastic as these AI tools and these LMs are, I think they struggle in terms of true

00:57:59.256 --> 00:58:00.536
creativity at times.

00:58:00.536 --> 00:58:04.215
And they excel at other things.

00:58:04.215 --> 00:58:07.975
They excel at writing code way faster than I'm ever going to be able to write code.

00:58:08.055 --> 00:58:13.256
And they excel at writing words and documents way faster than I would ever be able to do

00:58:13.256 --> 00:58:13.815
it.

00:58:13.815 --> 00:58:23.416
But I can come in, and what I've begun to realize is I can let myself engage in creative,

00:58:23.416 --> 00:58:30.136
curious work that kind of lets me not push aside, it lets me delegate, maybe is the right

00:58:30.136 --> 00:58:30.695
term, right?

00:58:30.695 --> 00:58:36.136
It lets me delegate some of the time consuming, maybe even tedious things where, OK, like

00:58:36.296 --> 00:58:37.735
I need to tune this plot the right way.

00:58:37.735 --> 00:58:40.776
And so I'm going to spend 20 minutes doing that when I couldn't spend that 20 minutes

00:58:40.776 --> 00:58:44.055
like thinking a little bit more deeply about the data and come to the realization that

00:58:44.055 --> 00:58:48.455
actually a different plot would be better here and would better communicate my point.

00:58:48.455 --> 00:58:51.896
And so I think it helps us kind of remove some of those constraints that previously

00:58:51.896 --> 00:58:55.815
prevented us from really being wildly creative in our exploration.

00:58:56.935 --> 00:59:02.536
And so if I give like the simplest answer to that question, I think it's our curiosity

00:59:03.496 --> 00:59:06.616
and our ability to be creative that we bring to the table.

00:59:06.616 --> 00:59:10.776
And that combined with the kind of like speed efficiency gains that these AI systems bring

00:59:12.055 --> 00:59:15.016
is, in my mind, the best of both worlds as it currently stands.

00:59:15.016 --> 00:59:16.376
Obviously, the world's changing rapidly.

00:59:16.376 --> 00:59:18.455
Who knows what models of tomorrow are going to look like?

00:59:18.455 --> 00:59:23.575
But today, I think this kind of combination of human creativity, ingenuity, determination,

00:59:23.575 --> 00:59:29.815
the things that kind of are inherently human, quote unquote, combined with the efficiency

00:59:29.815 --> 00:59:31.656
gains of these models is the sweet spot.

00:59:33.256 --> 00:59:35.656
All right.

00:59:35.656 --> 00:59:36.935
Well, that was fantastic.

